# The one process in front of Atrium. THIS FILE IS THE PRODUCTION ONE.
#
# For a plaintext localhost topology, see `deploy/Caddyfile.dev` and
# `docker-compose.dev.yml`. They are separate files rather than a variable in
# this one, because round 4 shipped this file with `:80` and a paragraph asking
# the operator to change it — and a comment is not a control. There is no
# spelling of this file that serves production over cleartext.
#
# It exists for three reasons:
#
#  1. **TLS terminates here, automatically.** The site address below is
#     `{$ATRIUM_DOMAIN}`, which Caddy resolves at load time: point the hostname
#     at this box, publish 80 and 443, and Caddy obtains and renews a
#     certificate over ACME on its own. `docker-compose.yml` declares
#     ATRIUM_DOMAIN with `${ATRIUM_DOMAIN:?...}`, so compose refuses to start
#     before Caddy ever sees an empty value — and Caddy refuses an empty site
#     address too, which is the second lock on the same door.
#     Caddy also serves the `:80` → `:443` redirect for this host itself; there
#     is no cleartext listener here that answers anything else.
#  2. **It is what makes the rate limiter's IP dimension real.** Both Atrium
#     processes read the caller's address from `X-Forwarded-For` with
#     `ATRIUM_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=1` — one proxy, this one, appending the peer
#     address on the right. Without something in this position the web app has
#     no address at all: a Next.js Server Action cannot see the socket, and Next
#     fills `x-forwarded-for` from the peer only when the client sent none, so a
#     present value there is unfalsifiable. Round 3 of issue #26 shipped exactly
#     that gap — `hops=0`, a null address, and a per-address limiter that
#     counted nobody.
#  3. It is the only service with a published port, so the hop count is a claim
#     nobody can walk around.
#
# `reverse_proxy` sets `X-Forwarded-For` by appending the immediate peer, which
# is the property the hop count depends on: the rightmost entry is written by
# this process and cannot be forged by a client. A client that sends its own
# `X-Forwarded-For` gets it prefixed, not honoured — `1.2.3.4, <real peer>` —
# and both limiters count the real peer.
{
	# Access logs go to the container's stdout with the rest of the stack.
	admin off
	# No `email` here on purpose: ACME registers fine without a contact address,
	# and an `email {$VAR}` that is unset makes Caddy fail on *that* line, which
	# hides the failure that matters (an empty site address below). Add
	# `email you@example.com` here if you want expiry notices.
}

# Loopback-only proxy liveness. This proves Caddy loaded its routes; the app's
# own healthcheck separately proves a real rendered page succeeds.
http://localhost:2019 {
	handle /caddy-health {
		respond "ok" 200
	}
	handle {
		respond 404
	}
}

# A hostname, not a port: this is what turns automatic HTTPS on. An IP address
# or a bare `:80` here would disable it, which is why the value is required
# rather than defaulted.
{$ATRIUM_DOMAIN} {
	log {
		output stdout
		format console
	}

	# HSTS. The certificate is automatic, so the downgrade path is the one thing
	# left worth closing: a year, on this host, after which a browser that has
	# been here once will not try cleartext again.
	header {
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
	}

	# The realtime server. WebSocket upgrades pass through untouched; `/health`
	# is here too so an external checker can reach it through the same entry.
	handle /ws* {
		reverse_proxy server:4000
	}
	handle /health {
		reverse_proxy server:4000
	}
	handle /attachments/* {
		reverse_proxy server:4000
	}
	# Path-style S3 URLs preserve /bucket/key. Keeping the path and Host intact
	# is part of the signature, so this is deliberately not `handle_path`.
	handle /{$S3_BUCKET}/* {
		reverse_proxy minio:9000
	}

	# Electric shapes (#201) — THROUGH THE APP, and there is no route from here
	# to `electric:3000`.
	#
	# The fallback `handle` below would send these to `app` anyway. This block is
	# written out regardless, because the thing worth being unable to typo is the
	# ABSENCE of a `reverse_proxy electric:3000` next to it. Electric authenticates
	# nobody: it answers any shape request for any table in its publication, with
	# any `where` the caller writes. A route straight to it would hand every
	# browser every room's document.
	#
	# So the browser's only path to a shape is this one, and it lands on
	# `apps/web/app/electric/v1/shape/route.ts`, which reads the session cookie
	# that rides this same-origin request, authorizes the room membership, and
	# composes the `where` clause server-side before forwarding. Electric's port
	# is published nowhere in docker-compose.yml, and its API secret is held only
	# by `app` — two more locks on the same door.
	handle /electric/* {
		reverse_proxy app:3000
	}

	# The Yjs write door (#202). A Yjs update is small; the app caps the body at
	# 4 MiB on the actual read, but the proxy caps it FIRST so an oversize or
	# unbounded (chunked, no Content-Length) body is refused before it ever
	# occupies the app process. `max_size` is enforced on the streamed body, not
	# a trusted header. Must precede the catch-all `handle` below — Caddy's
	# `handle` blocks are first-match, and this path is a subset of it.
	#
	# This matcher is deliberately narrow (PUT + exact path), and it does not need
	# to be exhaustive: the app-level `readBodyBounded` is the backstop, not this
	# cap. A trailing-slash or otherwise-shaped path falls to the catch-all and
	# still reaches `route.ts`, whose PUT handler caps the body on the actual read;
	# a non-PUT verb never has its body read at all — Next answers 405 for the
	# unimplemented method before any body is consumed. So the only thing this
	# proxy cap adds is refusing an oversize body one hop earlier for the common,
	# exactly-matched request; it is defence in depth, not the load-bearing guard.
	@ydoc_write {
		method PUT
		path /api/rooms/*/ydoc
	}
	handle @ydoc_write {
		request_body {
			max_size 4MB
		}
		reverse_proxy app:3000
	}

	# Everything else is the Next.js app: pages, Server Actions, the auth
	# catch-all route.
	handle {
		reverse_proxy app:3000
	}
}
